2308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 248 0 R
/T (Both are O\(1\) per entry, but the lookup avoids the arithmetic: it was about 2.9 times faster for Decimal and 1.9 times for float. The cost is O\(k\) extra space for k different pairs: the 1-decimal grid has k = 1,581 pairs \(about 393 KB measured\). It also needs O\(k\) time to build, so it only pays off when n is much larger than k: a single centre of 300 farmers makes 6,000 entries, but one block has 120,000. With 2-decimal readings \(Task 12\) k grows to 501 x 301 = 150,801 pairs, which at the same per-entry size would be roughly 37 MB \(an estimate, not a measurement\), and the table would be less attractive.)
/E (Both are O\(1\) per entry, but the lookup avoids the arithmetic: it was about 2.9 times faster for Decimal and 1.9 times for float. The cost is O\(k\) extra space for k different pairs: the 1-decimal grid has k = 1,581 pairs \(about 393 KB measured\). It also needs O\(k\) time to build, so it only pays off when n is much larger than k: a single centre of 300 farmers makes 6,000 entries, but one block has 120,000. With 2-decimal readings \(Task 12\) k grows to 501 x 301 = 150,801 pairs, which at the same per-entry size would be roughly 37 MB \(an estimate, not a measurement\), and the table would be less attractive.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
