2617 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 253 0 R
/T (With round\(\), 4.25 \(7.5 steps\) goes to 8 but 4.35 \(8.5 steps\) also goes to 8, because ties go to the even integer. Two readings 0.10 % apart are paid the same rate, while 4.35 and 4.36 \(0.01 % apart\) differ by a whole step. Counting the 501 readings from 2.00 to 7.00 shows the effect: the original rule gives steps alternately 9 and 11 readings wide, and none of the 51 steps has exactly 10; a half-up rule gives 10 readings to 48 of the 51 steps \(the three exceptions are the two end steps and step 0, which is symmetric about the standard\). A half-up rule, with ties moved away from zero, treats every step equally and is symmetric above and below the standard.)
/E (With round\(\), 4.25 \(7.5 steps\) goes to 8 but 4.35 \(8.5 steps\) also goes to 8, because ties go to the even integer. Two readings 0.10 % apart are paid the same rate, while 4.35 and 4.36 \(0.01 % apart\) differ by a whole step. Counting the 501 readings from 2.00 to 7.00 shows the effect: the original rule gives steps alternately 9 and 11 readings wide, and none of the 51 steps has exactly 10; a half-up rule gives 10 readings to 48 of the 51 steps \(the three exceptions are the two end steps and step 0, which is symmetric about the standard\). A half-up rule, with ties moved away from zero, treats every step equally and is symmetric above and below the standard.)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [94]
>>
endobj
